## What I found first

There is **no callable image generator here** — no MCP servers, no generation credentials, no local diffusion tooling. Stock sourcing was also mostly closed: Pexels' API needs a key, Unsplash serves a bot challenge to both curl and a real Chromium session, and Pexels' rendered gallery rate-limits after about two searches. I recorded all of it in `design-blocker.md`, along with the substitution I used instead of skipping the gate: both directions are **actual page compositions authored in HTML/CSS/SVG/canvas and rendered to PNG through headless Chromium** at 1440px and 390px. Real images of real designs — not prompts, not sourced references relabelled.

The garment imagery is authored too: nine drawn technical flats on one shared scale, which I redrew twice after inspecting them at plate size (the first pass read as icons, the second as coats). They never imply photographs of fictional inventory, and both designs say so on the page.

## The two directions

**A — The Unroll** (`design/a-*.png`). Ink field, Archivo pushed to 125% width for a 132px hero lockup, one vermilion for state only. One "cloth column" holds plate 01 in the hero and is the *same element* that later splits into the product grid — continuity is literal. A pinned stage scrub-scrubs nine authored crops (push in on the corozo buttons, pull back out), then FLIPs into the shop. All motion budget on one continuous camera; no video, no pixelation.

**B — Plate & Press** (`design/b-*.png`). A printed catalogue on paper: Instrument Serif against Roboto Mono captions, nine numbered plates on a ruled contact sheet. The focal device is a genuine halftone — a canvas routine samples each photograph, auto-levels it, and prints it back as ink dots, with the cell shrinking from coarse to fine as the image crosses the fold. That one is **already working in the mockup**, which is why the three resolve states in `b-1-opening.png` are rendered rather than described. Filtering re-registers the sheet; choosing a plate lifts it off the sheet into the opened view beside the bag.

Both carry the whole route — all nine garments with exact copy and sold-out sizes, the filter with counts and the All state, the bag with named lines and subtotal and the free-delivery gap, the full five-row chart, every care fact, all three shipping regions, the three reviews, the three shop facts.

**I recommend B**, and the reason is in its plan: its focal device is built rather than promised, its continuity sits inside the shopping task instead of in front of it, and it is the only one of the two that could absorb real photography later without a redesign — it already mixes drawn plates with halftoned photographs. A has the more spectacular opening but spends its budget on a runway before the first garment, and its FLIP across a sticky boundary is the riskiest thing in either plan.

One honest caveat on B's mockup: the frontispiece halftone is handsome but abstract — you can't quite read it as a collar. Fixable with a different crop in phase two.

`design-directions.json` is written in the required shape. Stopping here for your selection — tell me the id (or ask for a revision), and phase two builds the prototype slice first: for B, one plate resolving on real scroll and FLIPping into the opened view, with its entry and exit, at both widths.

