## What shipped

The catalogue-as-printed-object from the selected images, in the real app: masthead → frontispiece with the live index of nine plates → the device band → the sheet (filter + 3×3 plates) → opened plate beside the bag → spec sheet → care marginalia → shipping → reviews → colophon.

**The focal device is real.** `src/halftone.js` samples each photograph, auto-levels it and prints it back as ink dots on paper; scroll progress drives the dot cell down a quantised ladder from 26pt to a 3pt floor. One rAF loop and one scroll listener serve every canvas; only intersecting ones print; backing stores are DPR- and width-capped. I verified it under thrash — fast reversal coarsens it back (26pt), forward re-resolves (3pt), resize survives, no errors.

**Continuity sits inside the shopping task.** Filtering re-registers the sheet (surviving plates FLIP to their new cells, rects re-measured every run). Choosing a plate lifts the drawing out of its sheet cell and grows it into the enlarged view. Reduced motion prints everything at the finest cell immediately, cuts instead of re-registering, and skips the lift — the halftone stays, because it is the art direction, not the animation.

## Things I found and fixed by looking

- The frontispiece printed instantly at load, killing the device where it's introduced. Above-the-fold plates now get a timed press stroke instead of scroll travel.
- That fix had a real bug: the "already on screen" test compared against `lead`, so any lead above 1 matched everything and the band's first plate jumped to 100%. Now it tests the viewport.
- The lift animated from the plate's paper ground rather than the drawing, so the garment *shrank* into place instead of growing.
- Care marginalia numbers drifted off their statements; spec annotations collided with the drawing; 19px of horizontal overflow at 320px from those annotations.
- The finalize check caught two I had not: 10.5px mono on buttons was below the readability floor, and the masthead eyebrow collided with the wordmark.

## Tested

Desktop 1440, mobile 390 with touch, 320, 900, and reduced motion as a separate pass — each driving the full purchase: filter, pick a size, add, remove, check the bag. Sold-out sizes are disabled everywhere (Oxford XS, Chore S/XL, Trouser M, Crew L, Cardigan XS/S, Short XL). Delivery arithmetic verified both sides of the threshold ("£66.00 more for free delivery" → free over £100). Keyboard opens a plate and selects a size. No console errors in any pass; zero horizontal overflow at every width.

## Deviations worth naming

- **Product views are drawn, not photographed** — the consequence of the phase-1 finding that no image generator is callable here and stock search is gated. The four Pexels photographs appear only halftoned, as atmosphere, captioned "Representative image · not stock", credited in `src/assets/CREDITS.md`.
- **The filter bar is sticky on desktop only.** Below 620px it scrolls away; at two rows tall it cost too much screen, and the finalize check flagged it overlapping plate text.
- **Mobile spec annotations became a legend under the drawing** rather than hanging off it — there is no margin to hang them in at 320px.
- **The band's three captions are now live readouts** ("11pt dot · 44% inked") instead of the reference's static 0%/55%/100% labels, since in the real page those values are true and changing.
- The frontispiece crop is still fairly abstract at full print — the caveat I raised at the gate. I kept the selected image's composition rather than swapping the photograph unasked.

Implementation complete; human taste verdict: awaiting user review.